findings.body:
**Send SIGKILL to the process group after the leader closes within the grace window.**

In `terminateGracefully`, a SIGTERM that lets the leader close within `termGraceMs` returns `'terminated'` at `src/shared/proc.ts:720-721`. The SIGKILL rung runs only when that wait times out. A same-group descendant that ignores SIGTERM and redirects its stdio can therefore keep running after `child.close` resolves. The CLI transport still sets `aborted` for a governed run (`src/driver/claude-code/cli.ts:694-695`).

The defect is in the shared helper, not at `src/driver/claude-code/cli.ts:720-721`. Those lines handle stdout lines. Signal the group with SIGKILL after the leader closes:

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

```diff
   fireRung(onRung, 'sigterm');
   child.kill('SIGTERM');
   if (await closesWithin(termGraceMs)) {
+    child.kill('SIGKILL'); // reap any surviving same-group descendant
     return 'terminated';
   }
```

</details>

This group signal does not record a `sigkill` marker. Decide whether the narration should show it. The `child.exited` early return at `src/shared/proc.ts:713-715` skips group signalling as well. Apply the same group SIGKILL there.
